    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "I would compute that in an outer variable that I would then use :) I think that it creates a better readability. Also using && will propagate the value `false` which is probably not what we want.\n\n``` jsx\n    if (!showMore) {\n        return null;\n    }\n\n    const disabled = this.props.limitMin >= this.props.items.length;\n    const theme = disabled ?\n        applyTheme('showMore', 'showMore', 'showMoreDisabled'):\n        applyTheme('showMore', 'showMore');\n\n    return (\n        <button {...theme} >\n        </button>\n    );\n```\n",
    "commit_id": "f862c8e3bf89b51b66a7efc9ac9ab434583c8026",
    "created_at": "2016-10-28T12:57:39Z",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -80,14 +80,14 @@ class List extends Component {\n   renderShowMore() {\n     const {showMore, translate, applyTheme} = this.props;\n     const {extended} = this.state;\n-\n+    const disabled = this.props.limitMin >= this.props.items.length;\n     if (!showMore) {\n       return null;\n     }\n \n     return (\n       <button\n-        {...applyTheme('showMore', 'showMore')}\n+        {...applyTheme('showMore', 'showMore', disabled && 'showMoreDisabled')}",
